---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { layerGroups, incidents, costs, vulns, vulnNote, wpSupply, fairPoints, staticRisks, stats, checklist, matrix } from '../data/content';

const log = [
  ['sitemap-index.xml', 'generated'],
  ['404.html', 'generated'],
  ['og/index.png', 'generated'],
  ['_headers (CSP, HSTS)', 'shipped'],
  ['pages with a title + description', 'all of them'],
  ['trackers loaded before consent', '0'],
  ['cookies set before consent', '0'],
  ['plugins to patch', '0'],
];
const total = checklist.reduce((n, g) => n + g.items.length, 0);
const pageTitle = "It's Done. A static site, built properly.";
const pageDescription = 'A fair reply to "It Looks Done". The invisible 80% is real. It just does not require WordPress, and it does not require a plugin stack to patch.';
const siteUrl = Astro.site?.toString() ?? '';
const headSource = [
  `<title>${pageTitle}</title>`,
  `<meta name="description" content="${pageDescription.slice(0, 60)}…">`,
  `<link rel="canonical" href="${siteUrl}">`,
  `<meta property="og:image" content="${siteUrl}og/index.png">`,
].join('\n');
---
<Base title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
  <header class="top">
    <div class="draft" role="note">Draft. Security figures were checked against the linked sources on 2026-10-09. Costs and request/response snippets are illustrative.</div>
    <div class="bar">
      <div class="wrap">
        <a class="logo" href="/">It's Done <span class="mini-stamp">Passed</span></a>
        <span class="by">a reply, by Maciek Palmowski</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main id="main">
    <header class="hero">
      <div class="sky" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="stars s1"></span><span class="stars s2"></span><span class="stars s3"></span>
        <i class="cloud c1"></i><i class="cloud c2"></i><i class="cloud c3"></i>
        <i class="moon"></i>
      </div>
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">A reply, after dark</p>
        <h1 class="display">It's <em>done</em>.<span class="check-badge" aria-hidden="true">✓</span></h1>
        <p class="sub">Same site. Same Tuesday. Almost nothing to patch.</p>
        <p class="lede">
          The original page is right about a lot: a screenshot is not a launch. But it compares a vibe-coded app
          with WordPress and stops there. There is a third option, and it has been done since the build finished.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="btn" href="#timeline">Run the same day →</a>
          <a class="btn plain" href="#checklist">What's actually checked?</a>
        </div>

        <div class="log-wrap">
          <div class="log" role="img" aria-label="Build log: sitemap, 404 page and social image generated; no trackers loaded and no cookies set before consent, and zero plugins to patch. Condensed from this site's build.">
            <div class="head">$ npm run build</div>
            {log.map(([k, v]) => (
              <div class="row">
                <span>{k}</span>
                <span class={v === '0' ? 'zero' : 'ok'}>{v === '0' ? '0' : `✓ ${v}`}</span>
              </div>
            ))}
            <div class="row last"><span>done in 1.4s</span><span class="ok">exit 0</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="big-stamp" aria-hidden="true">Passed</div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="band" id="agreed">
      <div class="wrap split">
        <h2 class="display">The 80% is real.</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Sitemaps, 404s, social previews, metadata, consent, form handling, redirects, backups. A prompt that
            produces a pretty hero section does not produce those. WordPress core is well maintained, and the original is
            right that a fresh AI prompt will not give you roles, revisions and a sitemap by default.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where we disagree is the conclusion. That list is a <strong class="hl">checklist, not a platform</strong>.
            Almost every item on it can be built once, at build time, and then it stays done without a server
            running it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band concrete" id="waterline">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="band-head">
          <h2 class="display">Fourteen layers below the waterline.</h2>
          <p>The same fourteen the original lists, in its four groups. Here is where each one lives on a static site, what skipping it costs, and where a static site has to rent something.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="groups">
          {layerGroups.map((g) => (
            <div class="group">
              <div class="group-head">
                <h3>{g.name}</h3>
                <span class={`cost ${g.cost}`}>Cost to miss: {g.cost}</span>
              </div>
              <ul class="boxes">
                {g.layers.map((l) => (
                  <li class="box">
                    <h4>{l.name}</h4>
                    <p>{l.static}</p>
                    <p class="without"><b>Without it:</b> {l.without}</p>
                    {l.needs && <p class="needs"><b>Needs:</b> {l.needs}</p>}
                    {l.tryIt && <p class="try"><a href={l.tryIt.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{l.tryIt.label} ↗<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>}
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="timeline">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="band-head">
          <h2 class="display">Eight incidents. Three outcomes.</h2>
          <p>The original's eight incidents, in its order and at its times. The vibe and WordPress outputs follow what it shows. The static column and the notes are ours, and our snippets are illustrative; the links marked ↗ are live on this site.</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="rail">
          {incidents.map((i) => (
            <li>
              <time>{i.time}</time>
              <h3>{i.title}</h3>
              <div class="trio">
                <div class="vibe"><b>Vibe app</b>{i.vibe}<pre class="art">{i.vibeCode}</pre></div>
                <div class="wp"><b>WordPress</b>{i.wp}<pre class="art">{i.wpCode}</pre></div>
                <div class="static"><b>Static, built properly</b>{i.static}<pre class="art">{i.staticCode}</pre></div>
              </div>
              {i.note && <p class="note"><b>Note:</b> {i.note}</p>}
              {i.tryIt && <p class="try"><a href={i.tryIt.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{i.tryIt.label} ↗<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>}
              {i.options && (
                <div class="opts">
                  <h4>{i.options.heading}</h4>
                  <ul>
                    {i.options.items.map((o) => (
                      <li><a href={o.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>{o.name}</strong><span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a><span>{o.text}</span></li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                </div>
              )}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band concrete" id="receipt">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="band-head">
          <h2 class="display">"$0.00" leaves a few lines out.</h2>
          <p>
            The original prices the vibe side at $150 an hour of failure and the WordPress side at zero. WordPress
            itself is free. Hosting, premium plugins and the person who patches them are not. And a static site is not free
            either: give editors a CMS and you are paying for one, or maintaining one.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="receipts">
          {[['bad', costs.wp], ['good', costs.static], ['mid', costs.cms]].map(([tone, r]) => (
            <div class={`receipt ${tone}`}>
              <h3>{r.title}</h3>
              <p class="meta">illustrative, not a quote</p>
              <ul>{r.items.map((c) => <li><span>{c.item}</span><span>{c.range}</span></li>)}</ul>
              <p class="total li"><span>TOTAL</span><span>{r.total}</span></p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="small gap">{costs.note}</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band ink" id="plugins">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="band-head">
          <h2 class="display">The security team covers core. Plugins do the 80%.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="sentence">
          WordPress logged <em>{stats[0].n}</em> new vulnerabilities in 2025. <em>{stats[1].n}</em> were in plugins.
          Six were in core. Median time to first mass exploitation: <em>{stats[2].n}</em>.
        </p>
        <div class="prose lead-in">
          <p>
            Figures are from <a href="https://patchstack.com/whitepaper/state-of-wordpress-security-in-2026/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Patchstack's State of WordPress Security in 2026<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>, a security vendor, and my employer, so I have an interest in the topic.
            By their count, {stats[3].n} of 2025's vulnerabilities had no patch at disclosure. The original's "dedicated
            security team" covers core. The plumbing it lists (SEO, forms, consent, backups) comes from plugins.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Reported vulnerabilities, scrolls sideways on small screens">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>What was reported</th><th>Who can trigger it</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {vulns.map((v) => (
                <tr><td>{v.category}</td><td>{v.finding}</td><td><span class={`sev ${v.open ? 'High' : 'Medium'}`}>{v.access}</span></td><td><a href={v.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{v.source}<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="small gap">{vulnNote}</p>

        <h3 class="subhead">Supply-chain attacks hit WordPress too</h3>
        <ul class="points">
          {wpSupply.map((r) => (
            <li><strong>{r.title}</strong> {r.text} <a href={r.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{r.source} ↗<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="small gap">Both are the WordPress version of the npm risk below: you inherit whoever owns, or ships for, your dependencies.</p>

        <h3 class="subhead">What WordPress gets right</h3>
        <ul class="points">
          {fairPoints.map((f) => <li><strong>{f.title}</strong> {f.text}</li>)}
        </ul>

        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            The fairer version of the original's message: <em>WordPress covers the 80%, but only if someone owns
            plugin patching and monitoring.</em> That ongoing cost is the part the page leaves out. It is a cost, not
            a flaw: you can pay a host, an agency or yourself.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band concrete" id="static-risks">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="band-head">
          <h2 class="display">Static has its own risks.</h2>
          <p>Less server-side code is a smaller target, not no target. Here is what moves instead.</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="points">
          {staticRisks.map((r) => (
            <li><strong>{r.title}</strong> {r.text}{r.href && <> <a href={r.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{r.source} ↗<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></>}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="small gap">This site installs 204 packages to build and test it (9 direct) and none run on a server. Fewer, pinned dependencies and a committed lockfile make that surface easier to review.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="checklist">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="band-head">
          <h2 class="display">The original checks the plumbing. Check the safety too.</h2>
          <p>
            The original hands you six checks to run yourself. Here they are against this site, plus three it leaves out:
            whether what ships is patched, whether it is accessible, and what a stranger can reach. Open an example and its box ticks itself.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="checks">
          {checklist.map((g) => (
            <div>
              <h3>{g.group}</h3>
              <ul class="check">
                {g.items.map((c) => (
                  <li>
                    <label><input type="checkbox" /><span><b>{c.title}</b> {c.ask}</span></label>
                    <div class="artifact">
                      {c.links && <p class="links">{c.links.map((l) => <a href={l.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{l.label} ↗<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>)}</p>}
                      {c.kind === 'og' && (
                        <figure class="og">
                          <a href="/og/index.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the share image, opens in a new tab"><img src="/og/index.png" width="1200" height="630" alt="Share card for this site" loading="lazy" /></a>
                          <figcaption><b>{pageTitle}</b><span>{siteUrl.replace(/^https?:\/\//, '').replace(/\/$/, '')}</span></figcaption>
                        </figure>
                      )}
                      {c.kind === 'source' && (
                        <details>
                          <summary>Show the source</summary>
                          <pre class="term">{headSource}</pre>
                        </details>
                      )}
                      {c.kind === 'term' && (
                        <details>
                          <summary>Show the output</summary>
                          <pre class="term">{c.code}</pre>
                        </details>
                      )}
                      {c.note && <p class="small">{c.note}</p>}
                    </div>
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="checkfoot">
          <p class="score" id="score" aria-live="polite" data-total={total}>0 / {total} verified</p>
          <p class="passed" id="passed" aria-hidden="true">Passed</p>
        </div>
        <noscript><p class="small">The tally needs JavaScript. The list works without it.</p></noscript>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band concrete" id="built">
      <div class="wrap split">
        <h2 class="display">It runs its own checklist.</h2>
        <div>
          <div class="prose">
            <p>Same transparency as the original, different stack. AI helped with drafting, layout and code. A person chose the claims and checked the sources. These parts are build-time code, not plugins:</p>
          </div>
          <pre class="term gap">$ npm run build
  sitemap-index.xml ........ generated
  404.html ................. generated
  og/index.png ............. generated
  _headers (CSP, HSTS) ..... shipped
  trackers before consent .. 0
  cookies before consent ... 0   → anything added later stays gated</pre>
          <p class="small gap">The security headers and the 301 redirect take effect on hosts that read <code>_headers</code> and <code>_redirects</code> (Netlify, Cloudflare Pages). Other hosts need the same rules in their own config.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="matrix">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="band-head">
          <h2 class="display">Where's your line?</h2>
          <p>Pick the tool for the job. Sometimes that is WordPress.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Which tool for which job, scrolls sideways on small screens">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You need</th><th>Pick</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {matrix.map((m) => <tr><td>{m.need}</td><td><span class="pick">{m.pick}</span></td><td>{m.why}</td></tr>)}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band ink closing" id="cta">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="display">Done is a property of the build.</h2>
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>If your site is mostly pages, you can have all fourteen layers without running an application server of your own. The host serves the files, and free tiers exist. Add a CMS or functions and some of the risk and the bill come back, so budget for them.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="btn" href="https://docs.astro.build/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Build it static →<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
          <a class="btn plain" href="https://itlooksdone.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the original<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer class="site">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="foot-grid">
        <div class="foot-brand">
          <a class="logo" href="/">It's Done <span class="mini-stamp">Passed</span></a>
          <p class="small">A friendly reply to <a href="https://itlooksdone.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">itlooksdone.com<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>, by Maciek Palmowski. Not affiliated with its authors.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Full disclosure</h3>
          <p class="small">
            I work at <a href="https://patchstack.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Patchstack<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>,
            a security company, and I like both Astro and WordPress. The vulnerability numbers here come from
            Patchstack's data, so I'm not exactly neutral. This is my own side project, not an official Patchstack
            thing. The original is from the WebDevStudios crew, who build WordPress sites and make a fair point.
            Think of this as a friendly reply, not a fight. Do check the sources, though.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Privacy</h3>
          <p class="small">
            This page sets no cookies and loads no trackers. The <a href="/demo/consent">consent demo</a> sets one
            cookie, your choice, and only once you make it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </footer>

  <script src="../scripts/checklist.ts"></script>
</Base>
